cooking_oils {"look_for":["“Vegetable oil” on a label names a category, without any seed or fruit.","Read the label for the oil’s name first.","Look for refined or unrefined on the bottle.","A bottle that says only “vegetable oil” names no oil.","Choose tallow, olive oil, or coconut oil by the ingredient list.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Skip avocado oil too: the label cannot prove the oil is pure."],"watch_out":["Oil that starts smoking in the pan is no longer fit to use."]}
nut_butter_ingredients {"look_for":["The no-stir texture comes from added palm or hydrogenated oil, usually with sugar.","The list should read peanuts, and salt if you want it. Nothing else is needed.","Oil separated on top is the good sign. Nothing was added to hold it in.","Store natural jars upside down in the fridge. The oil redistributes on its own.","Same reading for almond, cashew and sunflower butters.","Read the jar’s ingredients for added oils; choose nuts and salt, with no added fat.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Reduced-fat versions usually replace the fat with sugar and starch.","Skip avocado oil added to a nut butter as well."],"watch_out":["A short ingredient list separates naturally and needs a stir before use. Palm oil or another added fat keeps a nut butter from separating on the shelf. That is what the ingredient line shows when it appears.","With no stabilizer in it, a natural jar left warm for a long stretch goes rancid. The fridge is where an opened jar belongs."]}
plant_butter {"look_for":["Plant butters differ in their oils, and the ingredient list names each one.","Choose coconut or olive oil in the ingredients, then check every other oil in the spread.","Check whether a nut milk or culture is listed, as in some versions.","Compare the fat line on the Nutrition Facts.","Skip seed oils and avocado oil, even when coconut or olive oil comes first."],"watch_out":["The name alone does not say which oils went in."]}
label_cold_pressed_expeller {"look_for":["Most supermarket oil is solvent-extracted, then refined, bleached and deodorized.","Read the oil name and ingredients first: choose olive or coconut oil. A cold-pressed seed oil stays a seed oil.","‘Expeller-pressed’ means mechanical, no solvent. Friction heat still happens.","‘Cold-pressed’ means pressed with the temperature held down. Tightly defined only for olive oil.","‘Refined’, ‘pure’, or no process word at all usually means solvent-extracted and refined.","Unrefined oils taste like their source. Refined ones taste like nothing, which is the point of refining them.","Dark bottle, cool shelf, and a size you will finish. Pressed oils are the least stable.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Skip avocado oil regardless of the pressing words; its label cannot prove purity."],"watch_out":["A pressed seed oil is still a seed oil. These words rank how the oil was made and say nothing about what it was made from.","No process word at all usually means solvent-extracted and refined.","“Cold-pressed” is tightly defined only for olive oil.","“Pure” and “refined” both describe oil stripped of color, flavor and aroma."]}
mayo {"look_for":["A jar sold on avocado or olive oil can carry other oils; the list shows which leads.","Check that the name on the jar is Mayonnaise.","Choose olive oil mayonnaise with egg yolk and vinegar or citrus juice. Check every oil in the ingredient list.","Compare jars by what they add beyond oil, egg yolk, acid and salt.","Once opened, keep it in the fridge and use it within 1 to 2 months.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Skip avocado oil mayo too, even if it names no other oil."],"watch_out":["A front label can name an oil that the ingredient list ranks lower."]}
hummus {"look_for":["Hummus bi tahina names its two parts, so chickpeas and tahini should lead the list.","Read the list for chickpeas and tahini at the top.","Look for lemon or citric acid; an acid balances the chickpeas and tahini.","A tub listing guar gum runs thicker; without it, water and grind set the texture.","Check the tub’s ingredients for added oil; choose olive oil or a hummus with no added oil.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Skip hummus made with avocado oil, even when the front calls the tub traditional."],"watch_out":["A grainy tub likely comes from a coarse grind of the chickpeas."]}
deli_salads {"look_for":["Cold food stays at 40°F or below, and egg, tuna or macaroni salad keeps 3 to 5 days.","Buy salads from a refrigerated case.","Check the Sell-By date, and skip a tub past it.","Get it home cold; two hours out at most, one hour above 90°F.","Read the salad’s ingredient label, including the dressing: choose olive oil or mayonnaise made with olive oil.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Skip avocado oil in the dressing as well. Ask for the ingredient list at the deli counter."],"watch_out":["Egg, tuna and macaroni salads keep 3 to 5 days; chicken salad, three to four.","Salads made with mayonnaise do not freeze well."]}
rotisserie_chicken {"look_for":["A solution bird was injected with water and seasoning before it went on the spit.","Sodium phosphate, a starch or a sweetener after the water means a solution bird. The shorter the seasoning line, the plainer the chicken.","A bird held under the lamp is held hot for a reason. Take one that is steaming, not one that has sat.","Check the sticker for oil in the seasoning; choose olive oil or a bird with no added oil.","Seed oils to skip: sunflower, safflower, canola, soybean, corn, and cottonseed.","Read the sticker, not the name. ‘Seasoned’, ‘original’ and ‘traditional’ are marketing; the ingredient list is regulated.","The shortest list wins: chicken, salt, spices. Water as the second ingredient means a solution was added.","Skip a bird seasoned with avocado oil too."],"watch_out":["FSIS: eat or refrigerate a hot rotisserie bird within 2 hours of purchase. If it will not be eaten in that window, cut it up. Refrigerate it in shallow covered containers.","Hot food is held at or above 140°F. A bird sitting in the case long enough to fall below that has left its hold. FSIS puts the growth range at 40 to 140°F.","Eat within 3 to 4 days, cold or reheated to 165°F. Freezing is fine; use within 4 months for texture."]}

